## ABSTRACT
The high toxicity and mobility of hexavalent chromium $(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI}))$ make it easy to spread and bioaccumulative, and its detection is a major part of environmental protection. In this work, an innovative method is developed for preparation of cerium oxide nanorod templated metal organic frameworks (CeO2NRs-MOF). The in-situ growth of MOF on the surface of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ nanorods $\mathrm{(CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs)}$ ) enhances its oxidase-like activity. In the presence of a trace amount of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ NRs-MOF can significantly accelerate the oxidation of $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) due to the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ -boosted oxidation, resulting in a blue colored oxidation product. It can detect $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ over a range of $0.03{\scriptstyle-5}\ \upmu\mathrm{M}$ with a high selectivity. Moreover, this method can be applied to the detection of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ in different water environment samples with satisfactory recoveries, demonstrating the potential application of CeO2NRs-MOF for the direct monitoring of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ in the environmental water system. Thus, this work provides a facile host-templated MOF preparation method, which makes it possible to further extend to other fields.
Keywords: chromium; cerium dioxide; metal organic frameworks; nanozyme; colorimetric method
## INTRODUCTION
Human activities include metal plating, leather tanning, prevention of corrosion and wood preservative treatment are responsible for elevated chromium levels in the environment.1 Chromium is present in the environment in two main oxidation states: $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , which have significantly different chemical properties and toxicity. Cr(VI) is highly toxic with a high mobility to jeopardize water quality.2 The maximum level of total chromium in water is $50~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}$ in the Guidelines for Drinking-water Quality of the World Health Organization.3 Therefore, it is imperative to develop rapid and sensitive methods for detecting $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ .
Analytical methods such as mass spectrometry,4,5 surface-enhanced Raman scattering spectroscopy,6,7 and chromatography,8-10 have high sensitivity and accuracy, but they often require a long sample pretreatment and complex operations. Colorimetric methods are attractive for their simplicity and on-site monitoring.11 In a recent study, Lu and coworkers proposed colorimetric quantification of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ based on gold amalgam.12 Analogously, gallic acid-capped gold nanoparticles were used to construct a colorimetric chromium chemosensor.13 Due to the strong Au-S bond between gold and thiol groups, gold nanomaterials can be combined with biological detection technology.14 However, the stability of nano-gold sol is seriously affected by environmental factors (pH, ionic strength, background electrolytes, and natural organic matter),15 thus affecting its subsequent use for quantitative detection of targets in environmental water.
Cerium oxide $\left(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\right)$ has been extensively utilized in catalysis, adsorption, and detection owing to its high concentration of oxygen vacancy, structure defects and excellent physicochemical properties such as dual oxidation states of $\mathrm{Ce(III)}$ and Ce(IV),16-18 which can also be useful as a nanozyme, such as oxidase,19 peroxidase,20 phosphatase,21 nuclease,22 and urease.23 $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ has many advantages over natural enzymes due to low cost, high stability in a wide range of temperatures, low toxicity and good biocompatibility.24-26 However, the nanozyme activity of pure $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ is relatively weak and it takes a long time to interact with the substrate, which is necessary to modify the surface of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ .27 As a cost-effective approach, various structures based on $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ were employed to enhance activity, such as the composite materials with noble metals (Au, Ag, and $\mathrm{Pt}$ ),28-30 and metal oxides ( $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}.$ ).31,32 Fluoride can also be used to enhance the oxidase-like activity of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ by modulating the surface charge and electron transfer.22 Coating a molecularly imprinted hydrogel shell can also enhance nanozyme activity of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ , meanwhile the specificity for substrate was greatly improved.33 Meanwhile, $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) is selected as the chromogenic substrate in this work. TMB is an extensively used chromogenic agent because of its reaction products with high absorption coefficients.34 TMB is a non-carcinogen that is more sensitive than typical chromogenic substrates such as 2,2-azinodi-(3-ethylbenzothiazoline sulfone-6)(diammonium salt) (ABTS) and o-phenylenediamine (OPD) for the catalytic oxidation of nanozymes.35,36
In this work, cerium oxide nanorods based metal-organic frameworks (CeO2NRs-MOF) were prepared by the sacrificial templating method. The metal oxide template could provide metal ions by sacrificing itself and then initiate the growth of MOF. The nucleation and growth of MOF on the template $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ were assisted by solvothermal reaction without high reaction temperature and complex operation. The as-synthesized CeO2NRs-MOF greatly improved the catalyzed oxidation efficiency of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ . Furthermore, the catalyzed oxidation of CeO2NRs-MOF for TMB was sensitive to the $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ from $30~\mathrm{nM}$ to $5~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ due to the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ -boosted catalyzed oxidation activity of CeO2NRs-MOF. The proposed colorimetric method features high sensitivity and selectivity with a limit of detection (LOD) for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ of $20\ \mathrm{\nM}$ , revealing the application prospect for the on-site determination of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ in real water samples.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Reagents and Instruments. Cerium(III) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and ceric ammonium nitrate $\mathrm{(Ce(NH_{4})_{2}(N O_{3})_{6})}$ were purchased from Saan Chemical Technology Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). Sodium hydroxide (NaOH), terephthalic acid (TA), 3,3’,5,5’-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), N,N-dimethylformamidel (DMF), sodium acetate (NaAc), acetic acid (HAc) and other common reagents were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). All the reagents were analytical grade and used without further purification. 18.2 MΩ·cm ultrapure water used throughout all experiments was obtained from a Milli-Q water purification system. The tap water was collected from Nanchang University. The morphology of the nanostructured materials was imaged by a D/MAX2200PC power X-ray diffraction instrument (Rigaku, Japan) with Cu Kα radiation and a JEOL 2010 transmission electron microscope (JEOL, Japan) with a $200~\mathrm{kV}$ accelerating voltage. UV-vis absorption spectrum was collected on a UV-2450 spectrophotometer (Shimadzu, Japan). Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (Bruker, Germany) had proved the functional group of the material. Raman spectra were collected using a Renishaw InVia micro-Raman (Renishaw, UK) system with the excitation wavelength at $532~\mathrm{nm}$ and the range of $300{-}1500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . Chemical composition was characterized by the VG Multilab 2000X X-ray photoelectron spectroscope (Thermo Scientific, USA).
Preparation of Cerium Based Metal-Organic Frameworks. 0.4340 g of $\mathrm{Ce(NO_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ and $4.8000\ \mathrm{g}$ of NaOH were dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of ultrapure water with stirred for half an hour, and then reacted at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in a high pressure reactor. The mixture was cooled to room temperature. After centrifugation $(8000~\mathrm{rpm}$ , $5~\mathrm{\min}\dot{}$ ), the product was washed three times with ultrapure water and ethanol respectively, then dried in a vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight to prepare the bright yellow $\mathrm{CeO_{2}N R s.^{37}0.0200\ g}$ of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ were weighed and mixed with $\ensuremath{1.4952\mathrm{~g~}}$ of TA dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. After the above solution reacted at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ in an autoclave, the mixture was cooled to room temperature. Then the product was centrifuged $5000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ , 5 min) and washed three times with DMF and ethanol respectively, and dried in a vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight to obtain the dark yellow CeO2NRs-MOF. As a comparison, $\phantom{-}0.1447\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Ce(NO_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ and $\ensuremath{0.4984\mathrm{~g~}}$ of TA were first dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and stirred at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This mixture was kept stirring for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ with the formation of a slurry. Consequently, the suspension was centrifuged (5000 rpm, 5 min) and washed three times with DMF, and dried in a vacuum oven at $70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain the white Ce(III)-MOF. The synthesis of $\mathrm{Ce(IV)}$ -MOF was similar to that of Ce(III)-MOF, except that $\mathrm{Ce(NO_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ was replaced by equal moles of $\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{6}$ . In order to prepare $\mathrm{Ce(IV)/Ce(III){-}M O F}$ , $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}\ 2.5~\mathrm{M}~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ solution was dropwise added to Ce(III)-MOF solution $\mathrm{\langle5\mg/mL\rangle}$ for ultrasonic dispersion. The product was centrifuged $5000\mathrm{rpm}$ , 5 min) and washed with ultrapure water until neutral, finally dried in a vacuum oven at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain the yellow powder.
$\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathbf{\mathop{VI}})$ Sensing. Colorimetric sensing of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ was performed to determine the lowest concentration detection ability and the selectivity of the TMB/Cr(VI) system. During colorimetric detection, different concentrations of each $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from 0.03 to 5 $\upmu\mathrm{M}$ in the NaAc-HAc buffer solution ( $\mathrm{100~mM}$ , pH 4) were added into TMB ( $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $20~\mathrm{mM})$ and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ -MOF ( $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $5~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ ) mixed solution, adding water to the total volume of $500~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ . It was estimated the lowest limit of detection by the naked eye and characterized by UV-vis spectrophotometer. The color of the solution immediately turned blue, indicating that oxTMB was generated and the characteristic absorption peaks appeared at 370 and $650~\mathrm{nm}$ in the UV-vis spectrum. In the same way, the selectivity of the synthesized $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ NRs-MOF was tested against various cations and anions in the above-mentioned system, where the concentration of $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{VI})$ is one tenth of that of other ions ( ${\mathrm{1~\upmuM:10~\upmuM}}$ . These studies were used to prove that CeO2NRs-MOF has good performance detection and selectivity for the TMB/Cr(VI) system.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
$\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathbf{\mathop{VI}})$ for Boosting $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ -catalyzed Oxidation. In general, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can provide strong oxidizing hydroxyl radicals (•OH) to accelerate the oxidation of chromogenic substrates in nanoceria catalyzed oxidation systems,38 in which •OH mainly originates from the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ catalyzed decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}.{}^{20,39}\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ also has oxidase-like activity and can oxidize TMB without $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , but the catalytic efficiency is very low taking tens of minutes.40,41 It may be that nanoceria is easy to aggregate and unstable in water.42 Furthermore, both $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ and TMB possess positive charges at acidic conditions, resulting in incomplete oxidation due to electrostatic repulsion.43 Efforts have recently been made to improve the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ -catalyzed oxidation, such as forming hybrid nanostructures of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ and noble metals.39,44 In this study, to enhance $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ -catalyzed oxidation activity, Ce-MOF is generated in situ on the surface of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ to form CeO2NRs-MOF. Meanwhile, $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ can accelerate the catalyzed oxidation of CeO2NRs-MOF, changing the color of TMB in a very short time $(<30~\mathrm{s}$ ) (Figure 1A). The oxidase-like activity of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ and CeO2NRs-MOF in the presence of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ is demonstrated by UV-vis absorption spectrum. As shown in Figure 1B, in the case of the addition of ${5}~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}~\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , the characteristic absorption peaks of oxTMB produced by $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ NRs-MOF catalytic oxidation are significantly stronger than that of the system without the addition of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . The above facts show that $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ is easily reduced to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ on the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}.$ NRs-MOF, thus replacing the common $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -induced Fenton effect.45 At the same time, comparing the absorption spectra of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NRs-MOF, it is proved that CeO2NRs-MOF has a stronger catalytic oxidation efficiency than $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ under the accelerated reaction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ .
Characterization of $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}\mathbf{NRs}$ -MOF. In order to demonstrate the formation of core-shell structure by in-situ growth of Ce-MOF on the surface of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ , transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was used. As shown in Figure 2A, the length of the prepared $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ is about $120~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . With the TA induced the dissolution of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ and surface-directed nucleation and growth of Ce-MOF, an outer shell of Ce-MOF approximately $2\cdot5\ \mathrm{nm}$ is observed (Figure 2B), while the shape of the CeO2NRs-MOF is not change significantly. Moreover, as shown in Figure 2C, except for those the characteristic peaks of a fluorite-like face-centered cubic $(\mathrm{Fm}{-}3\mathrm{m})$ ) $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ phase (JCPDS 34-0394), the residual peaks at $2\uptheta=8.2^{\circ}$ , $17.7^{\circ}$ , $18.4^{\circ}$ , $20.3^{\circ}$ and $23.7^{\circ}$ are attributable to the Ce-MOF.46,47 Available from the comparison curves, the coverage of the Ce-MOF does not change the basic crystal structures of the template $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ . TEM and power X-ray diffraction pattern reveal that the core-shell structure was formed by in-situ growth of Ce-MOF on the surface of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ with distinct crystal structures.
The chemical composition of the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ NRs-MOF was further characterized by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), which illustrated XPS full survey spectra (Figure S1A) and high-resolution XPS spectra of different elements (Figure 3). The full spectra confirm the existence of Ce, O in the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ and CeO2NRs-MOF. As shown in Figure S1A, the O/Ce ratio of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ is 3.2, while O/Ce ratio of CeO2NRs-MOF is 4.9, indicating that Ce ions are combined with the carboxyl group of the ligand TA to form a MOF structure and introduce O element. Then the relationship between different binding groups and different binding energies is further studied by high-resolution XPS spectra. As shown in Figure 3A, the two peaks locate at binding energy of 884.2 and $902.4~\mathrm{eV}$ in the high-resolution Ce 3d XPS spectrum are the result of a Ce(III) $3\mathrm{d}^{9}4\mathrm{f}^{1}$ O $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{p}}^{6}$ final state. Furthermore, the binding energy peaks of 897.6 and $916.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ are the result of a Ce(IV) $\mathrm{3d^{9}4f^{0}}$ O $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{p}}^{6}$ final state, while the binding energy peaks of 881.6, 888.1, 900.1, and $906.4\mathrm{eV}$ are the result of $\mathrm{Ce(IV)}$
$3\mathrm{d}^{9}4\mathrm{f}^{2}$ O $\mathsf{2p^{4}}$ and Ce(IV) $3\mathrm{d}^{9}4\mathrm{f}^{1}$ O $2\mathrm{p}^{\mathrm{5}}$ final states.48-50 Similar to ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}{\mathrm{NRs}}$ , the high-resolution Ce 3d XPS spectrum of CeO2NRs-MOF shows the eight peaks at binding energy of 884.1, 902.3, 881.7, 887.9, 897.6, 900.1, 906.4 and $916.0\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Figure 3C), indicating that the synthesized CeO2NRs-MOF has Ce(III) and Ce(IV). Likewise, comparing Figure 3B with Figure 3D, the O 1s signals at 528.7 eV $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs})$ and $528.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ ( $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NRs-MOF) associate to the Ce(III)-O interaction, and those at $530.5\ \mathrm{\eV}$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs})$ and 530.7 eV (CeO2NRs-MOF) associate to Ce(IV)-O respectively,51 which are also confirmed by the Ce-O vibration modes at 482 and $744~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in the Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (Figure S1B, compared with $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ , $0.0{\cdot}=0$ vibration modes at 1393 and $1558~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ of CeO2NRs-MOF show obvious blue shift, which proves the formation of Ce-OOC units).52 Raman spectrum of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ -MOF provides further information about the structure (Figure S1C). The main peak centered at about $455\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ corresponding to $\mathrm{F}_{2\mathrm{g}}$ symmetric vibration of fluorite structure $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ is exhibited, indicating that the structure of the template $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ is still retained in CeO2NRs-MOF. The peak at $606~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is attributed to oxygen vacancy caused by existence of $\mathrm{Ce(III)}$ . The peaks at 858, 1135 and $1430~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ are assigned to vibration of Ce-O, $\mathrm{o}\mathrm{-}$ and COO units, respectively.53,54 Compared with CeO2NRs-MOF, the Raman spectra of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ shows no characteristic peaks of Ce-MOF except two characteristic peaks at 455 and $606~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . The above results confirmed the presence of oxygen vacancy in the crystal lattice.55,56 The oxygen vacancy of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}]$ NRs-MOF is closely related to the easy conversion of its oxidation state, as well as its oxidase-like catalytic activity. On the other hand, the UV-vis absorption spectrum of CeO2NRs-MOF is broad and weak in the range of $240{-}300~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S1D), which is consistent with the ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) mechanism of MOF covalent linkage,57,58 indicating that cerium ions bind to the carboxylic acid ligands to form the Ce-O. During the synthesis reaction of CeO2NRs-MOF, the free Ce(IV) with a high oxidation potential of $1.5\mathrm{V}$ is easily reduced to $\mathrm{Ce(III)}$ .59 So the abundant $\mathrm{Ce(IV)}$ (from the core) and Ce(III) (from the core and the reduction of $\mathrm{Ce(IV)}\mathrm{\Gamma}$ ) in the MOF in-situ generation process are dissolved, then binding to the ligand TA in the solution. The Ce(IV)/Ce(III)/TA complexes are then self-assembled on the surface of the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ to form a core/shell structure. On the basis, Ce(III)-O/Ce(IV)-O ratios are calculated by integral area from the high-resolution O 1s XPS spectrum (Figure 3B and 3D). This Ce(III)-O/Ce(IV)-O ratio is 0.3874 in the CeO2NRs-MOF, much higher than 0.2641 in the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ (Table S1), proving that the proportion of Ce(III) is increased in CeO2NRs-MOF, which is beneficial to its catalytic activity.56 These studies support the successful preparation of CeO2NRs-MOF with a high proportion of $\mathrm{Ce(III)}$ and oxygen vacancies and enhanced oxidase-like activity.
Catalyzed Oxidation Activity of $\mathbf{CeO}_{2}\mathbf{NRs}$ -MOF. To systematically evaluate the catalyzed oxidation activity of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ -MOF, the Ce(III)-MOF, Ce(IV)-MOF, Ce(IV)/Ce(III)-MOF, and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ were prepared and further studied by the catalytic oxidation of TMB as a time-dependent model reaction. As shown in Figure 4A and 4B, the time-dependent absorbance changes reveal that the MOFs with only Ce(III) or Ce(IV) lack the capacity for catalytic oxidation. Furthermore, as illustrated in Figure 4C and 4D, Ce(IV)/Ce(III)-MOF and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ have the oxidase activities due to the mixed valence of Ce, but the catalytic oxidation rates for the substrate TMB are very low. Figure 4E shows that when the reaction time reached one minute, the catalyzed oxidation of CeO2NRs-MOF for TMB has reached a higher level under the concentrations of Cr(VI): 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, $2~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , and $0.4~\mathrm{mM}$ TMB in the NaAc-HAc buffer (100 mM, pH 4)). Ce(III)-MOF (A); Ce(IV)-MOF (B); Ce(IV)/Ce(III)-MOF (C); $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ (D); CeO2NRs-MOF (E). The oxidase activity of different types of Ce-based nanomaterials with $5~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ Cr(VI) in $0.4~\mathrm{mM}$ TMB and NaAc-HAc buffer (100 mM, pH 4) (F).
promotion of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , after which the degree of reaction increases with time but tends to be gentle, while the catalyzed oxidation of other Ce-based nanomaterials are still in the relatively high-speed initial reaction stage during this time. As a result shown in Figure 4F, the highest activity is observed with the CeO2NRs-MOF, which is 4.2 times and 8.7 times higher than those of the Ce(IV)/Ce(III)-MOF and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ , respectively. So, the order of catalyzed oxidation activity is CeO2NRs-MOF $>$ $\mathrm{Ce(IV)/Ce(III)\mathrm{-}M O F>C e O_{2}N R s>>C e(I I I)\mathrm{-}M O F~o r}$ Ce(IV)-MOF. It is well known that the catalyzed oxidation activity of $\mathrm{Ce}$ -based nanomaterials is closely linked to the special double valences-redox cycle $\mathrm{(Ce(IV)/Ce(III))}$ and oxygen vacancies.56 Meanwhile, the strong oxidability of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ is more likely to take electrons from substrate and can facilitate exchange electron, accelerating the redox cycle of the co-existence of $\mathrm{Ce(IV)}$ and $\mathrm{Ce(III)}$ sites in Ce-based nanomaterials.40 Unlike Ce(IV)/Ce(III)-MOF and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ , the in-situ Ce-MOF of CeO2NRs-MOF protects the active site and greatly enhances its catalytic oxidation activity, indicating the potential application of CeO2NRs-MOF in $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ colorimetric detection.
$\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathbf{\mathop{VI}})$ Selectively Enhanced TMB Oxidation. Selective experiments were carried out to evaluate the effect of metal ions for the TMB oxidation system. The system was added the follow ions: blank, $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ , $\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Pr}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Gd}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ho}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Lu}}^{3+}$ , F-, Cl-, Br-, $\mathrm{NO}_{3}\cdot$ , ${\mathrm{SO}}_{4}{}^{2\cdot}$ -, As(III), As(V) and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ (10-fold of other ions relative to $1~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ $\mathrm{Cr(VI)})$ . As shown inset of Figure 5, in the presence of these interfering species, no obvious color changes are observed by the naked-eye colorimetric assay. Furthermore, tolerance experiments in the presence of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ mingled with 28 different metal ions were carried out (Figure S2). The results show that the system is unaffected by the addition of foreign ions in the detection of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . As a comparison, the same selective experiments are verified using $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ and Ce(IV)/Ce(III)-MOF. In the presence of all ions including $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , the absorbance differences of TMB are almost constant (Figure S3), demonstrating that the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NRs-MOF shows promise for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ sensing with excellent selectivity compared to $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NRs}$ and Ce(IV)/Ce(III)-MOF. To further confirm the acceleration effect of metal ions for the system, the catalytic activities of CeO2NRs-MOF are studied by steady-state kinetics. The Michaelis-Menten curves are obtained for CeO2NRs-MOF in the varied concentrations of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ or $\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ (Figure S4). The initial velocity is calculated using the Beer-Lambert Law $(\varepsilon{=}39000~\mathrm{M}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , ${\sf b}{=}0.5$ cm). The Michaelis-Menten constant $(K_{\mathrm{m}})$ and maximum initial velocity $\mathrm{(V_{max})}$ are obtained from Lineweaver-Burk plots of the Michaelis-Menten equation $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{V=V_{max}}$ [S]/ $\left(K_{\mathrm{m}}\mathrm{+}[\mathrm{S}]\right)$ . $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ is the key parameter of enzyme affinity to substrates. A lower $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ represents the stronger the affinity between the enzyme and the substrate.60 The Michaelis-Menten constant $(K_{\mathrm{m}})$ and maximal reaction velocity $\mathrm{(V_{max})}$ are listed in Table 1. The experimental results show the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ has the lowest $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ and highest $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{max}}$ compared with the others, which are also the reason for the rapid response and high selectivity of this system. Moreover, the redox potential of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)/\mathrm{{Cr}(I I I)}}$ (1.36 V) is higher than other ions (such as $0.77\mathrm{~V~}$ of Fe(III)/Fe(II), 0.15 V of $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)/\mathrm{{Cu}(I)}}$ and $0.68\mathrm{~V~}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{\backslash}$ ). The higher redox potential indicates that is more likely to take electrons from substrate, which is also consistent with the conclusion of steady-state kinetics. These studies demonstrate that $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ can selectively boost the oxidase-mimicking activity of ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}{\mathrm{NRs-MOF}}$ .
