exploit optimal colorimetric performance for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , the absorbance difference between $650~\mathrm{nm}$ and 750 nm (ΔA) was monitored to evaluate the optimal reaction conditions. As shown in Figure S5A, the concentrations of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}.$ NRs-MOF affect the catalytic activity of CeO2NRs-MOF, and the optimal experimental condition of $0.2~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ is determined from the experiments and adopted as the standard condition for subsequent reactions. An increasing trend is observed for TMB concentration below $0.4~\mathrm{mM}$ (Figure S5B), which is chosen as the optimum concentration. A similar trend is also observed for the pH of NaAc-HAc buffer before pH 4 (Figure S5C). It has been reported that TMB is first oxidized to the intermediate product TMB radical (the single electron oxidation) at $\mathrm{pH}2–4$ , which forms the blue product with the significant characteristic absorption peaks (370 and $650~\mathrm{nm}$ ), then the radical is oxidized to quinonediimine at $\mathrm{pH~}4{-}7$ . While $\mathrm{pH}>7$ , TMB is oxidized in one step to become an azo compound, So pH 4 is a critical state.61-63 In fact, the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from aqueous media by nanoceria are highly pH dependent, under acidic conditions, nanoceria has a strong adsorption effect on $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , which may be related to the presence of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in solution. $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ species can be represented in different forms in solution, such as $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{CrO}_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ . In the acidic solution, the first-order dissociation constant $\left(\mathsf{p K a}_{1}\right)$ of chromic acid is 4.1, and the $\mathfrak{p K a}_{2}$ is 5.9.64,65 Thus, that is found mostly in dissociated form $\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}\mathrm{\cdot}$ in acidic buffer solution with $\mathrm{pH}4$ , which has strong oxidizing property and can promote TMB oxidation. When the pH value increases to neutral or even basic, the oxidizing property gradually weakens until it disappears, which is not conducive to TMB oxidation. So, the optimal experimental condition of $\mathsf{p H}4$ is chosen. As the Figure S5D shows the activity of CeO2NRs-MOF is almost unchanged between $10{-}60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , indicating that this nanozyme has a wider temperature range than those of biological enzymes. For evaluating the applicability of CeO2NRs-MOF sensing platform for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ detection, the linear response range and limit of detection (LOD) were obtained under the above-mentioned optimum experimental conditions. As shown in Figure 6A, the absorption peaks at $650~\mathrm{nm}$ and $370~\mathrm{nm}$ increase with increase of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ concentration, accompanying TMB shown blue color of different shades. With the increase of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ concentration, the blue color of TMB solution is gradually deepened. Figure 6B shows the absorbance changes (ΔA) as functions of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ concentration. The linear range for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ detection is from 0.03 to $5~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ $\mathrm{R}^{2}{=}0.994)$ with a LOD $20~\mathrm{nM}$ $(\mathrm{LOD}=3\upsigma/\mathrm{S}$ , where S is the slope of the calibration curve, and $\upsigma$ is the standard deviation of the tests without $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , which is comparable with those methods reported previously (Table S2 and S3). Although the LOD is lower than that of AAS, the maximum level of total chromium in water is $0.96~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ in the Guidelines for Drinking-water Quality of the World Health Organization, which is much higher than our detection limit and within the detection range, indicating that our method is suitable for the detection of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in the water environment.
