Application to Real Samples. To validate the applicability of the proposed method to real samples, the quantification of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was carried out in tap water, Ganjiang river water, and Poyang lake water, respectively. Sediments from these real water samples were treated by filtration using a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ membrane to ensure the applicability of the proposed methods for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ determination. As listed in Table S4, the recoveries of spiked $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ with various concentrations (100, 500, 1000 nM) are tested in triplicate. The recovery efficiencies within $15\%$ are achieved, demonstrating a good reproducibility of this system for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ detection. The catalytic activity of CeO2NRs-MOF can still maintain around $95\%$ after three months. The high reproducibility and stability of the proposed method to real samples is an excellent candidate for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ determination in environmental water samples.
## CONCLUSIONS
In this work, for the first time, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NRs-MOF was synthesized by the sacrificial template method. The obtained CeO2NRs-MOF is a new type of host-templated MOF material with the high catalyzed oxidation activity for TMB. Furthermore, the oxidation of TMB can be significantly accelerated by a trace amount of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ during CeO2NRs-MOF-catalyzed oxidation. Under optimal conditions, the CeO2NRs-MOF-catalyzed oxidation system exhibits a good linear relationship with Cr(VI) concentrations over a range of $30\mathrm{\nM-}5\mathrm{\\upmuM}$ , and a detection limit of $0.02~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ can be achieved. The applicability of the developed $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}.$ NRs-MOF was confirmed by the results obtained from real water samples, demonstrating the great potential of this system for trace $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ detection in water environment. The findings of this work pave a new way to develop the synthesis of nanomaterials with nanozyme activity for analytical applications.
